3	//! `CedarAuthorizer`: the `Authorizer` port's implementation (ADR-0013, spec §7) — composes
4	//! the compiled-policy snapshot (Task 13), the entity-slice loader/cache (Task 12/14), the
5	//! generation-keyed decision cache (Task 14), and an [`AuditSink`] into the one component
6	//! `is_authorized` callers reach for. AppState wiring (`main.rs`/`adapters::http::AppState`)
7	//! is a separate follow-up task — this module only builds and unit-tests the authorizer
8	//! itself.
9	//!
10	//! **`is_authorized` flow (spec D11/D12, AC1):**
11	//! 1. Best-effort synchronous [`PolicySnapshot::reload_if_stale`] — a grant made moments ago
12	//!    is visible to THIS decision without waiting out the background poll interval (AC1). An
13	//!    `Err` (e.g. a transient store hiccup) is logged and swallowed: reload failures never
14	//!    fail a decision, they just mean this call evaluates against the last-known-good
15	//!    snapshot.
16	//! 2. Read the current compiled-policy snapshot via [`PolicySnapshot::current`] — a cheap,
17	//!    in-memory `Arc` clone that never errors. Its `r#gen` is the authoritative policy
18	//!    generation THIS call will evaluate against, and it is the ONLY source of the
19	//!    decision-cache key's policy component: it is never re-derived from a second,
20	//!    independently-timed [`GenerationsReader::policy_gen`] read. That second read used to
21	//!    be able to observe a concurrent policy bump the just-taken snapshot predates, minting
22	//!    a decision-cache key one generation NEWER than the policy set actually evaluated —
23	//!    caching a decision under the wrong generation until TTL. Deriving the key's policy
24	//!    component from `compiled.r#gen` itself closes that window: key and evaluated policy
25	//!    set are always the same generation, by construction.
26	//! 3. Read the entity generation counter via [`GenerationsReader::entity_gen`] to complete
27	//!    the decision-cache key. If it errors (the Redis-backed counter is unreachable), the
28	//!    cache is bypassed entirely for this call — no key, no `get`, no `put` — and evaluation
29	//!    proceeds unconditionally (D11/D12's fail-open property: an accelerator outage costs
30	//!    latency, never correctness). If it succeeds and the key is already cached, that cached
31	//!    [`Decision`] is returned immediately WITHOUT touching the audit sink again — the
32	//!    original miss that populated the cache already recorded the one audit event for this
33	//!    exact question.
34	//! 4. On a miss (or a bypassed cache), the authoritative path: load the [`EntitySlice`] for
35	//!    `(resource, principal)` and decide via [`PolicyEngine::decide`] against the SAME
36	//!    compiled snapshot read in step 2 (never a second `snapshot.current()` read). An
37	//!    `AuthzError::ResourceNotFound` slice-load error — the request names a tenancy node that
38	//!    doesn't (or no longer does) exist, reachable via the direct
39	//!    `POST /v1/authz/is-authorized` API with an arbitrary caller-supplied `resource_prn` —
40	//!    is caught here and turned into a fail-closed `Deny` (marked
41	//!    [`RESOURCE_NOT_FOUND_MARKER`]) rather than propagated: never a 500, and never an
42	//!    existence oracle that would let an unauthorized caller distinguish "denied" from
43	//!    "doesn't exist". Every OTHER slice-load error still propagates unchanged — it's a
44	//!    genuine backend failure (the request can't be decided at all), never swallowed.
45	//! 5. Record one [`AuthzDecisionEvent`] via the injected [`AuditSink`].
46	//! 6. Best-effort populate the decision cache (only if step 3 computed a key).
47	//!
48	//! **Timestamps:** [`AuthzDecisionEvent::at`] is stamped with `chrono::Utc::now()` directly
49	//! rather than through an injected `Clock` port. `CedarAuthorizer` doesn't otherwise need a
50	//! clock, and every existing `Clock` consumer in this crate (`adapters::clock::SystemClock`)
51	//! exists to make a *tested* time-dependent computation (JWKS TTL expiry, etc.)
52	//! deterministic; nothing here asserts on `at`'s value, so adding a clock dependency here
53	//! would be DI ceremony without a corresponding test benefit. If a future task needs to
54	//! assert audit timestamps precisely, threading a `Clock` through here is the natural
55	//! extension.
